description:
FILED s473t4 (#1501 CHECK 1 round 1, 2026-09-13).  t/run/runenv.t is TRANSPILE-FAIL: P 106/0, C 0/0 -- the whole file, 106 rows.

    $ ./pl2cl < t/run/runenv.t
    Parser2 TODO: self-referential init: my %base_opts = ( PERL_PERTURB_KEYS => $mode, PERL_HASH_SEED_DEBUG => 1 ),

THE SITE, t/run/runenv.t:246-247 -- a comma where a semicolon was meant, in perl's own source:
    for my $mode ( 0,1, 2 ) {
        my %base_opts = ( PERL_PERTURB_KEYS => $mode, PERL_HASH_SEED_DEBUG => 1 ),
          my ($out, $err) = runperl_and_capture( { %base_opts }, [ @print_keys ]);

MINIMAL REPRODUCER (probed vs perl 5.40.3).  NOTE t/run/runenv.t has NO `use strict` -- that is load-bearing:
    # no strict
    sub cap { my ($h, $a) = @_; return (join(",", map "$_=$h->{$_}", sort keys %$h), "@$a") }
    for my $mode (0, 1) {
        my %base_opts = ( M => $mode, D => 1 ),
          my ($out, $err) = cap( { %base_opts }, [ 'x' ] );
        print "mode=$mode out=[$out] err=[$err]\n";
    }
    perl: mode=0 out=[] err=[x]
          mode=1 out=[] err=[x]
    pcl : Transpile failed: Parser2 TODO: self-referential init: my %base_opts = ( M => $mode, D => 1 ),
              my ($out, $err) = cap( { %base_opts }, [ 'x' ] );

PERL'S RULE, and the whole finding: a `my` variable is not visible until the END of the statement that declares it.  The two `my`s here are ONE statement (joined by the comma operator), so `{ %base_opts }` in the second half does NOT see the `my %base_opts` being declared in the first -- it reads the PACKAGE variable `%main::base_opts`, which is empty.  perl prints `out=[]`.  With `use strict` perl rejects the same code ("Global symbol %base_opts requires explicit package name") -- probed both ways.

PCL's `self-referential init` detector sees the declared name inside its own statement and dies.  The detector is RIGHT that the name occurs there; it is wrong about what it means.  The fix is the same rule perl states: a name declared by a `my` in this statement resolves, for the REST OF THE SAME STATEMENT, to the outer/package variable -- which is also the correct answer for the genuinely self-referential `my $x = $x` case the detector was written for (Pl/Parser2.pm:9227).

SIZE: one statement-level scope rule, and the whole of run/runenv.t (106 rows) is behind it.  Round t5d (run/).
